<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<pretext xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<docinfo/>
<article xml:id="workshop-quick-reference">
<title>PreTeXt Workshop — Quick Reference</title>
<introduction>
<p><em>Everything you need during today's session.</em></p>
<assemblage><title>Before You Start</title>
<p>Open <url href="https://pretext.plus"><c>pretext.plus</c></url> in your browser. You will create a free account in the first ten minutes of the workshop.</p>
</assemblage>
</introduction>

<section xml:id="s1"><title>1. Create Your Account and First Project</title>
<p><em>We'll do this together.</em></p>
<ol>
<li><p>Go to <c>pretext.plus</c> and click <term>Sign up</term>. Use an email you can check right now.</p></li>
<li><p>After confirming your email, click <term>Projects</term>, then the blue <term>New project</term> button.</p></li>
<li><p>Give your project a title (e.g. <c>My First Lesson</c>) and click <term>Create</term>.</p></li>
<li><p>Click the gray <term>Edit</term> button. The editor opens with source on the left and a preview on the right.</p></li>
</ol>
</section>

<section xml:id="s2"><title>2. Learn the Rebuild Rhythm</title>
<p>The preview does not update automatically. Click the green <term>Rebuild</term> button (top-right of the preview), or press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>. This also saves your work.</p>
<p><alert>Edit → Rebuild → look at the preview.</alert></p>
</section>

<section xml:id="s3"><title>3. Practice with Five Short Exercises</title>
<p>For each exercise, find the closing <c>&lt;/section&gt;</c> tag and paste the snippet just above it. Then Rebuild.</p>

<subsection><title>Exercise 1 — Paragraph with inline math</title>
<program language="xml"><code><![CDATA[<p>The Pythagorean theorem states that <m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>,
where <m>c</m> is the length of the hypotenuse.</p>]]></code></program>
</subsection>

<subsection><title>Exercise 2 — A definition</title>
<program language="xml"><code><![CDATA[<definition xml:id="def-right-triangle">
  <statement>
    <p>A <term>right triangle</term> is a triangle
    with one angle measuring exactly 90 degrees.</p>
  </statement>
</definition>]]></code></program>
</subsection>

<subsection><title>Exercise 3 — An example</title>
<program language="xml"><code><![CDATA[<example>
  <title>A 3-4-5 triangle</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Show that a triangle with sides 3, 4, and 5
    is a right triangle.</p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>Check: <m>3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 = 5^2</m>.</p>
  </solution>
</example>]]></code></program>
</subsection>

<subsection><title>Exercise 4 — An exercise</title>
<program language="xml"><code><![CDATA[<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>Find the hypotenuse of a right triangle with
    legs of length 5 and 12.</p>
  </statement>
</exercise>]]></code></program>
</subsection>

<subsection><title>Exercise 5 — A cross-reference</title>
<program language="xml"><code><![CDATA[<p>For a reminder of the terminology,
see <xref ref="def-right-triangle"/>.</p>]]></code></program>
</subsection>
</section>

<section xml:id="s4"><title>4. Your Target — A Sample Lesson</title>
<p><em>After the break, build a short lesson on a topic you actually teach, shaped like the example below.</em></p>
<p><alert>Safety net:</alert> if stuck, paste the block below and change the topic.</p>
<program language="xml"><code><![CDATA[<title>The Product and Quotient Rules</title>

<p>When a function is built by multiplying or dividing
two simpler functions, we cannot just multiply or
divide their derivatives.</p>

<definition xml:id="def-product-rule">
  <statement>
    <p>The <term>product rule</term> states that if
    <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are differentiable, then
    <me>
      \frac{d}{dx}\bigl[f(x)\,g(x)\bigr]
      = f'(x)\,g(x) + f(x)\,g'(x).
    </me></p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<example>
  <title>Applying the product rule</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Find the derivative of <m>h(x) = x^2 \sin(x)</m>.</p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>Let <m>f(x) = x^2</m> and <m>g(x) = \sin(x)</m>.
    Then <me>h'(x) = 2x \sin(x) + x^2 \cos(x).</me></p>
  </solution>
</example>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>Use the product rule
    (see <xref ref="def-product-rule"/>) to find
    the derivative of <m>p(x) = x^3 e^x</m>.</p>
  </statement>
</exercise>]]></code></program>
</section>

<section xml:id="s5"><title>5. Share Your Lesson</title>
<ol>
<li><p>Save (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>) and click <term>Back to projects</term>.</p></li>
<li><p>Click your project, then <term>Sharable link</term>.</p></li>
<li><p>Copy the URL from your browser. Paste it in the shared document.</p></li>
</ol>
</section>

<section xml:id="s6"><title>6. If You Get Stuck</title>
<p><alert>Red error:</alert> missing or mismatched tag. Check line numbers in the error.</p>
<p><alert>Preview didn't update:</alert> click Rebuild or press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>.</p>
<p><alert>Lost your project:</alert> click Projects in the top nav.</p>
<p><alert>Something else:</alert> raise your hand, or ask a neighbor first.</p>
</section>

<section xml:id="s7"><title>7. After the Workshop</title>
<p>Free PreTeXt.Plus accounts give you 10 projects. For multi-file projects, images, or version control, ask for the take-home Codespaces guide. Community and documentation: <url href="https://pretextbook.org"><c>pretextbook.org</c></url>.</p>
</section>
</article>
</pretext>
